WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.926
Tokens per second measures generation rate, while prompt-processing speed measures input evaluation.

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:11.413
Time to first token and end-to-end latency matter for interactive use.

00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:15.144
Throughput matters for batches and multiple users.

00:00:15.144 --> 00:00:19.753
Splitting one model can increase capacity without increasing speed.

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:24.362
Each token may cross device boundaries or require synchronization.

00:00:24.362 --> 00:00:31.605
If communication dominates compute, adding a GPU can leave performance unchanged or make it worse.

00:00:31.605 --> 00:00:37.531
PCI Express bandwidth and topology affect movement among CPU memory and GPUs.

00:00:37.531 --> 00:00:42.140
Devices may share lanes or connect through different paths.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:47.188
Fast peer-to-peer features are hardware, platform, driver, and runtime dependent.

00:00:47.188 --> 00:00:53.992
GPU utilization should be interpreted with memory, clocks, power, temperature, transfer, and queue data.

00:00:53.992 --> 00:01:02.552
One GPU at low utilization may be waiting for another device or for communication rather than lacking work.

00:01:02.552 --> 00:01:06.722
Thermal throttling and power limits reduce sustained performance.

00:01:06.722 --> 00:01:10.453
Test long enough to reach stable temperatures.

00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:17.257
A short benchmark after a cold boot can hide airflow imbalance and power behavior.

00:01:17.257 --> 00:01:24.500
Measure the intended workload: model, quantization, context, batch, users, prompt length, output, and service layer.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.109
Compare single GPU, different splits, replicas, and separate-job placement.

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:33.279
Multi-GPU earns its complexity only through measured benefit.

00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:37.010
Report variability and percentiles, not only averages.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:46.448
Synchronization stalls, model loading, cache growth, queue bursts, and thermal behavior may create occasional long responses that dominate user experience.
